course: dictionary
lesson: 6983
title: Finding meaning in a place
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A word can name a place in a story, and the sentence around it shows you what kind of place it is. «The passage ended under the trap-door that led up into the butler's pantry.» A passage is a tunnel, and you climb up through a trap-door, so the pantry is a room at the top of that tunnel.
explain: «And the passage comes up under the butler's pantry, right next to the dining-hall!» Next to the dining-hall tells you the pantry is part of a house, close to a room where people eat.
explain: Pantry has more than one meaning, so check the sentence to choose the right one: a room or large cupboard where food is kept, a food pantry where people in need get free food, or (informally, in Australia, New Zealand and the UK) any kitchen cupboard used for food.
fact: A pantry is a small room or large cupboard in a home where food is kept.
fact: Tins, jars, flour and cereal are the kinds of food kept in a pantry.
fact: A food pantry is a place where people who need help can get free food.
fact: Informally, in Australia, New Zealand and the UK, any kitchen cupboard for food can be called a pantry, also called a larder.
fact: Phrases you may see: «stock the pantry», «pantry shelf», «raid the pantry» and «food pantry».
exercise: «They come to a trap-door, push it up together and climb into the butler's pantry.» Where do the children climb into? | A bus stop | *A pantry | A garden shed | A school hall
exercise: Which of these would you most likely find in a pantry? | Goldfish swimming in a bowl | A bed and two pillows | *Tins of beans and a bag of flour | A bicycle and a football
exercise: Ella's mum says, «We need to stock the pantry before the holidays.» What does stock the pantry mean? | *Fill it with food | Sweep the floor of the room | Lock the door and hide the key | Paint the room a new colour
test: Which of these is a food pantry? | A park for bikes | A room for sleeping in | *A place where people who need help can get free food | A shop that sells toys
test: Where is the butler's pantry in «the passage comes up under the butler's pantry, right next to the dining-hall»? | Under the sea | In a shop window | *In a house, near the dining-hall | In a school playground
exam: Which meaning of pantry fits «the butler's pantry» in the stories? | A place where free food is given to people in need | Any kitchen cupboard used for food, informally in the UK | A bus stop | *A small room or large cupboard in a home where food is kept
